{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>My dwelling in their midst</b> — on condition that I dwell in their midst. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 29:46). But this idiomatic use of the lamed as a condition of this sort is not found elsewhere. It is possible that he means: \"And they shall know, by My dwelling in their midst, that I am YHWH their Elohim who brought them out of the land of Egypt\" — that is, they shall know My glory and believe that I brought them out of the land of Egypt. This is like the usage of \"And David behaved himself wisely in all his ways\" (I Samuel 18:14), \"For you are chosen, O son of Jesse\" (ibid. 20:30), \"Because you rebelled against My word at the waters of Meribah\" (Numbers 20:24), and similar verses. But Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 29:46) that the meaning is: \"For I did not bring them out of the land of Egypt except in order that I may dwell in their midst,\" and this is the meaning of \"you shall serve Elohim upon this mountain\" (Exodus 3:12). He explained it well. And if so, there is a great sod in the matter, for according to the plain sense of the text, the Shekhinah's dwelling in Israel is a need of the ordinary person and not a need of the Most High; but it is like the matter that Scripture says: \"Israel, in you I will be glorified\" (Isaiah 49:3), and Joshua said: \"And what will You do for Your great Name?\" (Joshua 7:9), and many verses come likewise: \"It has been His desire for a dwelling place\" (Psalms 132:13), \"Here will I dwell, for I have desired it\" (ibid. 132:14), and it is written: \"And I will remember the land\" (Leviticus 26:42)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The phrase צֹרֶךְ הֶדְיוֹט וְלֹא צֹרֶךְ גָבוֹהַ ('a need of the ordinary person and not a need of the Most High') is a rabbinic idiom; 'hediot' and 'gavoah' are rendered here in their technical sense contrasting human need with divine self-sufficiency."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:16.365558220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}